[38;5;231m⏺[39m Current state: a minimal CommonJS Node project — [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m with no dependencies or scripts, [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m +
  [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m (a [38;5;153mgreet[39m helper), no React, no build tooling, no tests. So this is a new subsystem from scratch, not a
  change to an existing flow.

  A few questions before I propose approaches. Starting with the one that determines the most downstream.

  [1mThe fork:[0m where the todos live decides whether this is a browser-only app or a client plus a server, and that choice
  drives the whole build — tooling, testing, deployment, and how much work a later change costs.

  - [1mIn-memory only[0m — state resets on refresh. Cheapest by far, nothing to configure. Fine for a demo or a UI exercise;
    cheap to upgrade to localStorage later.
  - [1mlocalStorage[0m — survives refresh, still zero backend. Costs one small persistence module and a bit of test setup.
    Rules out sharing across devices/browsers, but that's cheap to add later behind the same interface.
  - [1mBackend API + database[0m — real persistence and multi-device. Costs a server, a schema, API contracts, and roughly
    triples the build. Hard to undo the added complexity if you don't need it.

  [1mMy recommendation: localStorage.[0m It gets you a todo list that actually behaves like one across refreshes, keeps the
  whole thing a single deployable static app, and if you ever want a server you swap one module rather than restructure.
  A backend is the one option that's expensive to walk back.
